cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "PhantomLoad: measuring the gap between enrolled and delivered demand response capacity"
message: "If you use this repository or its findings, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
    affiliation: "independent"
version: 0.2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/ASM-21/phantomload"
date-released: "2026-09-05"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - demand response
  - grid reliability
  - utility economics
  - capacity market
  - EIA-861
abstract: >-
  US utilities report an average of 39.2 percent of their enrolled demand
  response peak-reduction capability as actually realised at system peak over
  2013 to 2024. PhantomLoad measures that shortfall from EIA-861 aggregates and
  then shows it cannot be read as waste, on three independent grounds. The data
  cannot separate capacity that was never called from capacity that would not
  have delivered, and a twelve-year panel can only rule out reserve resources
  designed for events more frequent than once in 4.5 years. Priced against PJM's
  published cost of new entry, demand response wins six of the eight defensible
  pairings of accounting bases, including both pairings that mix nothing. And
  mix-adjusted reported capacity per enrolled customer varies five-fold across
  census divisions, enough room for inconsistent reporting to hold the entire
  shortfall. What decides the economics is when demand response is dispatched,
  which neither the retail nor the wholesale accounting reports.
